<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="sec-partial-derivatives">
	<title>Partial derivatives</title>

	<subsubsection>
		<title>Definition and basic concepts</title>

		<p>
			A partial derivative measures change in one input coordinate while the other input coordinates are held fixed.
		</p>

		<definition xml:id="def-u3-partial-derivatives">
			<title>Partial Derivatives</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>f(x,y)</m> be a scalar-valued function in two variables. The <term>partial derivative of <m>f</m> with respect to <m>x</m></term> at <m>(a,b)</m> is the scalar quantity
					<me>  f_x(a,b) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(a+h,b) - f(a,b)}{h}</me>,
					provided this limit exists. Similarly, the <term>partial derivative of <m>f</m> with respect to <m>y</m></term> at <m>(a,b)</m> is the quantity
					<me>f_y(a,b) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(a,b+h) - f(a,b)}{h}</me>
					provided this limit exists.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>

		<p>
			We compute the partial derivative in <m>x</m> of an expression precisely by treating the <m>y</m>-variable as a constant, and differentiating in <m>x</m>. Similarly, we compute the partial derivative in <m>y</m> by treating the <m>x</m>-variable as a constant.
		</p>

		<note>
			<statement>
				<p>
					There are several notations used for the partial derivative of a function <m>f(x,y)</m>, namely, we might write <m>f_x(a,b)</m> as
					<me>
						\partial_x f(a,b) \quad \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(a,b) \quad (D_x f)(a,b) \quad \text{or} \quad (D_1 f)(a,b)
					</me>.
					Similarly, we write <m>f_y(a,b)</m> as
					<me>
						\partial_y f(a,b) \quad \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}(a,b) \quad (D_y f)(a,b) \quad \text{or} \quad (D_2 f)(a,b)
					</me>.
				</p>
				<p>
					The symbol <m>\partial</m> is a stylized lowercase Latin <m>d</m>, not a Greek letter. It is pronounced <q>partial,</q> so <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m> is read <q>partial <m>f</m> over partial <m>x</m>.</q>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</note>

		<example>
			<title>Partial derivatives with one variable held constant (U3-LO3)</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let
					<me>f(x,y)=x^2-3xy+2y^2-4x+5y-12.</me>
					Find <m>f_x(x,y)</m> and <m>f_y(x,y)</m> by differentiating with respect to one variable while keeping the other variable constant.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					To find <m>f_x(x,y)</m>, hold <m>y</m> constant. We use the sum, power, constant-multiple, and constant rules from <xref ref="tab-u3-derivative-reminders"/> in <xref ref="sec-u3-general-functions-domains"/>. The term <m>-3xy</m> has constant coefficient <m>-3y</m>, while <m>2y^2</m>, <m>5y</m>, and <m>-12</m> are constant terms. Differentiating term by term gives
					<md>
						<mrow>f_x(x,y) \amp=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x,y)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp=2x-3y+0-4+0-0</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp=2x-3y-4.</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
				<p>
					To find <m>f_y(x,y)</m>, hold <m>x</m> constant. We use the same rules, now differentiating with respect to <m>y</m>. The term <m>-3xy</m> has constant coefficient <m>-3x</m>, while <m>x^2</m>, <m>-4x</m>, and <m>-12</m> are constant terms. Differentiating term by term gives
					<md>
						<mrow>f_y(x,y) \amp=\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}(x,y)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp=0-3x+4y-0+5-0</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp=-3x+4y+5.</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>

		<activity>
      <title>Computing first partial derivatives (U3-LO3)</title>
			<introduction>
				<p>
					For each function <m>f(x,y)</m>, find <m>f_x(x,y)</m> and <m>f_y(x,y)</m>.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>f(x,y) = 3x^2y+2x-3y</m>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<p>
						To find <m>f_x(x,y)</m>, treat <m>y</m> as a constant:
						<md>
							<mrow>f_x(x,y) \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(3x^2y+2x-3y)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = 3 \cdot 2xy + 2 - 0</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = 6xy + 2</mrow>
						</md>.
					</p>

					<p>
						To find <m>f_y(x,y)</m>, treat <m>x</m> as a constant:
						<md>
							<mrow>f_y(x,y) \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(3x^2y+2x-3y)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = 3x^2 \cdot 1 + 0 - 3</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = 3x^2 - 3</mrow>
						</md>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>f(x,y) = x\sin y + y\sin x</m>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<p>
						Treating <m>y</m> as a constant, we find that
						<md>
							<mrow>f_x(x,y) \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x\sin y + y\sin x)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = 1 \cdot \sin y + y \cdot \cos x</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = \sin y + y\cos x</mrow>
						</md>.
						Treating <m>x</m> as a constant, we find that
						<md>
							<mrow>f_y(x,y) \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(x\sin y + y\sin x)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = x \cdot \cos y + 1 \cdot \sin x</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = x\cos y + \sin x</mrow>
						</md>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>f(x,y) = x^2e^{xy}</m>.
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<p>
						To find <m>f_x(x,y)</m>, treat <m>y</m> as a constant and use the product and chain rules:
						<md>
							<mrow>f_x(x,y) \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x^2e^{xy})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x^2) \cdot e^{xy} + x^2 \cdot \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(e^{xy})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = 2x \cdot e^{xy} + x^2 \cdot e^{xy} \cdot \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(xy)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = 2x \cdot e^{xy} + x^2 \cdot e^{xy} \cdot y</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = 2xe^{xy} + x^2ye^{xy}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = xe^{xy}(2 + xy)</mrow>
						</md>.
						To find <m>f_y(x,y)</m>, treat <m>x</m> as a constant:
						<md>
							<mrow>f_y(x,y) \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(x^2e^{xy})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = x^2 \cdot \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(e^{xy})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = x^2 \cdot e^{xy} \cdot \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(xy)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = x^2 \cdot e^{xy} \cdot x</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = x^3e^{xy}</mrow>
						</md>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</activity>

    <activity xml:id="act-u3-sympy-first-partials">
      <title>Reading first partial derivatives in SymPy (U3-LO3)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          In <xref ref="act-u2-reading-row-reduction-code"/>, SymPy kept matrix calculations exact. It can also differentiate symbolic expressions. The command <c>sp.symbols("x y")</c> creates two independent symbols, and <c>sp.diff(f, x)</c> differentiates <c>f</c> with respect to <c>x</c>, holding <c>y</c> fixed. Recall the function <m>f(x,y)=3x^2y+2x-3y</m> from our hand calculations.
        </p>
        <p>In SymPy, <c>**</c> denotes exponentiation; for example, <c>x**2</c> represents <m>x^2</m>.</p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import sympy as sp

x, y = sp.symbols("x y")
f = 3*x**2*y + 2*x - 3*y
fx = sp.diff(f, x)
fy = sp.diff(f, y)

print((fx, fy))
print((fx.subs({x: 1, y: 2}), fy.subs({x: 1, y: 2})))
]]></code></program>
        <p>Output:</p>
        <program language="text"><code><![CDATA[(6*x*y + 2, 3*x**2 - 3)
(14, 0)
]]></code></program>
        <p>The command <c>.subs({x: 1, y: 2})</c> substitutes <m>x=1</m> and <m>y=2</m> into an expression.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Match the two expressions in the first output line to <m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m>. Verify each by hand.</p></li>
          <li><p>What is held fixed in each derivative? Interpret the two numbers in the second output line.</p></li>
          <li><p>What would <c>sp.diff(f.subs({x: 1, y: 2}), x)</c> return? Why does this fail to compute <m>f_x(1,2)</m>?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Holding <m>y</m> fixed gives <m>f_x=6xy+2</m>; holding <m>x</m> fixed gives <m>f_y=3x^2-3</m>. Thus <m>f_x(1,2)=14</m> and <m>f_y(1,2)=0</m>. These are the instantaneous rates of change at <m>(1,2)</m> when <m>x</m> varies with <m>y=2</m>, and when <m>y</m> varies with <m>x=1</m>, respectively.
        </p>
        <p>
          Substituting both coordinates into <m>f</m> first gives the constant <m>f(1,2)=2</m>. Its derivative with respect to <m>x</m> is <m>0</m>. To evaluate a partial derivative at a point, differentiate the expression first and then substitute the coordinates.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <p xml:id="p-u3-prediction-loss-partials">
      For the prediction loss in <xref ref="act-u3-prediction-loss-model"/>, each input coordinate is a model weight. A partial derivative will measure how the total squared error changes when we adjust one weight while keeping the other weight and the observed data fixed.
    </p>
	</subsubsection>

	<subsubsection>
		<title>Geometric interpretation</title>

		<definition>
			<title>The Slice of a Function</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Given a function <m>f(x,y)</m> and <m>b \in \R</m>, the <term>slice</term> of the function <m>f</m> in the plane <m>y = b</m> is the function <m>g: \R \to \R</m> given by <m>g(x) = f(x,b)</m>. Similarly, for <m>x \in \R</m> the slice of <m>f</m> in the plane <m>x = a</m> is the function <m>g: \R \to \R</m> given by <m>g(y) = f(a,y)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>

		<p>
			Geometrically, the slice of a function <m>f</m> in the plane <m>y = b</m> corresponding to intersecting the surface given by the equation <m>z = f(x,y)</m> with the plane <m>y = b</m>, creating a curve in this plane.
		</p>

		<example>
      <title>Sketching slices of a surface (U3-LO1)</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Sketch the slices of the function <m>f(x,y) = \sin(x + y^2)</m> in the plane <m>y = b</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					The slice in the plane <m>y = b</m> is the function
					<me>
						g(x) = f(x,b) = \sin(x + b^2)
					</me>.
					This slice is a sine function with a phase shift of <m>b^2</m>. See <xref ref="figu-8-5-graph-sin-x-y2-slices"/> for a sketch of these slices.
				</p>

				<figure xml:id="figu-8-5-graph-sin-x-y2-slices">
					<caption>The graph of <m>f(x,y) = \sin(x + y^2)</m> showing slices at different values of <m>y</m>. Each curve represents the function <m>f(x,y)</m> for a fixed value of <m>y</m>. Adapted from: Stanford's MATH 51 textbook</caption>
					<image source="figures/unit3/u3-slices-sin-x-plus-y2.png" width="100%">
						<shortdescription>
							A surface z equals sin of x plus y squared with several fixed-y slice curves.
						</shortdescription>
						<description>
							<p>
								The figure shows a translucent blue surface for <m>z = \sin(x+y^2)</m> over <m>0 \leq y \leq 1</m>. Five colored curves lie on the surface at fixed values <m>y=0</m>, <m>y=0.25</m>, <m>y=0.5</m>, <m>y=0.75</m>, and <m>y=1</m>, illustrating how the slice <m>z=\sin(x+b^2)</m> shifts as <m>b</m> changes.
							</p>
						</description>
					</image>
				</figure>
			</solution>
		</example>

		<theorem xml:id="thm-8-5-geometric-interpretation-of-partial">
			<title>Geometric Interpretation</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					If we consider the surface in <m>\R^3</m> described by the equation <m>z = f(x,y)</m>, then

					<ul>
						<li><m>f_x(a,b)</m> is the <em>slope of the tangent line</em> to the curve formed by intersecting the surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m> with the vertical plane <m>y = b</m>.</li>
						<li><m>f_y(a,b)</m> is the <em>slope of the tangent line</em> to the curve formed by intersecting the surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m> with the vertical plane <m>x = a</m>.</li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</theorem>

		<activity>
      <title>Partial derivatives as slopes (U3-LO3)</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>f(x,y) = 16 - x^2 - y^2</m>. Find <m>f_x(1,2)</m> and <m>f_y(1,2)</m> and interpret these numbers as slopes of a certain curve.
				</p>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					We calculate that
					<md>
						<mrow>f_x(x,y) \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(16 - x^2 - y^2)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = 0 - 2x - 0</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = -2x</mrow>
					</md>.
					So
					<me>f_x(1,2) = -2(1) = -2</me>.
					This is the slope of the curve in the <m>xz</m> plane obtained by intersecting the surface <m>z = 16 - x^2 - y^2</m> with the plane defined by the equation <m>y = 2</m>, at the point <m>(1,2,11)</m>. That is, the tangent line of the curve at this point is given by <m>z - 11 = -2 ( x - 1 )</m>.
				</p>

				<p>
					We calculate that
					<md>
						<mrow>f_y(x,y) \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(16 - x^2 - y^2)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = 0 - 0 - 2y</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = -2y</mrow>
					</md>.
					So
					<me>f_y(1,2) = -2(2) = -4</me>.
					This is the slope of the curve in the <m>yz</m> plane obtained by intersecting the surface <m>z = 16 - x^2 - y^2</m> with the plane defined by the equation <m>x = 1</m>, at the point <m>(1,2,11)</m>. That is, the tangent line of the curve at this point is given by <m>z - 11 = -4 ( y - 2 )</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>

		<activity xml:id="act-8-5-ex-contour-red-line">
      <title>Reading partial derivatives along a horizontal line (U3-LO3)</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Consider the contour plot shown in <xref ref="fig-8-5-contour-plot-example-increment-point2"/> for a function <m>F</m> with contour lines at increments of <m>0.2</m>. In particular, consider the curve obtained from the slice <m>y = 0</m>, indicated by the red line in <xref ref="fig-8-5-contour-plot-example-increment-point2"/>. By interpreting the contour plot, what can we say about the slopes of this curve, or equivalently, the values of <m>F_x</m> on this line?
				</p>

				<figure xml:id="fig-8-5-contour-plot-example-increment-point2">
					<caption>A contour plot for a function with increments of <m>0.2</m>. The line <m>y = 0</m> is indicated in red. Adapted from: Stanford's MATH 51 textbook</caption>
					<image source="figures/unit3/u3-partial-contours-y0-line.png" width="70%">
						<shortdescription>
							A contour plot with level increments of 0.2 and the line y equals 0 highlighted.
						</shortdescription>
						<description>
							<p>
								The contour plot shows a synthetic function with labeled level curves spaced by <m>0.2</m>. A thick red horizontal line marks <m>y=0</m>, so the intersections of that line with the contour curves can be compared with the one-variable slice along <m>y=0</m>.
							</p>
						</description>
					</image>
				</figure>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					Here is some of the information we can read from the contour plot:

					<ul>
						<li>Between <m>x = -1</m> and <m>x = 0</m>, the contour values increase (from about <m>0.2</m> to <m>1</m>), indicating <m>F_x > 0</m> in this region (the function <m>F</m> is increasing).</li>
						<li>Near <m>x = 1</m>, the contour lines are very close together, and the contour values decrease, indicating the <m>F_x \lt 0</m>, and that the magnitude of <m>F_x</m> is large (the curve slopes steeply downward).</li>
						<li>Between <m>x = 1.5</m> and <m>x = 3</m>, the contour values increase, indicating <m>F_x > 0</m>. (the function <m>F</m> is increasing).</li>
					</ul>

					See <xref ref="fig-8-5-slice-red-line"/> for a graph depicting the curve, to check what we have deduced is correct.
				</p>

				<figure xml:id="fig-8-5-slice-red-line">
					<caption>The slice of the function <m>F(x,y)</m> where <m>y = 0</m>. The dashed lines correspond to increments of <m>0.2</m> in the <m>x</m>-axis. Adapted from: Stanford's MATH 51 textbook</caption>
					<image source="figures/unit3/u3-partial-slice-y0.png" width="60%">
						<shortdescription>
							A red one-variable slice curve F of x comma 0 with dashed vertical guide lines.
						</shortdescription>
						<description>
							<p>
								The graph shows the slice <m>F(x,0)</m> as a red curve. Black points mark where the slice reaches contour levels in increments of <m>0.2</m>, and gray dashed vertical lines run from the <m>x</m>-axis up to those points.
							</p>
						</description>
					</image>
				</figure>
			</solution>
		</activity>

		<activity>
      <title>Reading partial derivatives along a vertical line (U3-LO3)</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Consider <xref ref="fig-8-5-contour-ex-2"/>, which is the same contour plot as in <xref ref="fig-8-5-contour-plot-example-increment-point2"/>, but with the vertical line <m>x = 2.25</m> indicated instead. As in <xref ref="act-8-5-ex-contour-red-line"/>, read off the behaviour of the partial derivatives <m>F_y</m> from the contour plot.
				</p>

				<figure xml:id="fig-8-5-contour-ex-2">
					<caption>A contour plot with increments of <m>0.2</m>, with the vertical line <m>x = 2.25</m> highlighted in red. Adapted from: Stanford's MATH 51 textbook</caption>
					<image source="figures/unit3/u3-partial-contours-x225-line.png" width="70%">
						<shortdescription>
							A contour plot with level increments of 0.2 and the line x equals 2.25 highlighted.
						</shortdescription>
						<description>
							<p>
								The contour plot shows the same synthetic function as the preceding contour figure, with labeled level curves spaced by <m>0.2</m>. A thick red vertical line marks <m>x=2.25</m>, so changes in contour values along this vertical slice can be read from bottom to top.
							</p>
						</description>
					</image>
				</figure>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					Here is some information that can be deduced from the contour plot:

					<ul>
						<li>
							The contour values are always decreasing as we increase <m>y</m> along the red line, so that <m>F_y \lt 0</m> on this line.
						</li>

						<li>The contour lines are closest together near <m>y = -1</m>, so <m>F_y(2.25,y)</m> will have largest magnitude on the line near <m>y = -1</m>.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</activity>

	<p>
		Generally, the spacing of contour lines corresponds to the magnitude of partial derivatives, and the sign of the partial derivatives corresponds to whether the function increases or decreasing in that direction.
	</p>

	<activity>
    <title>Checking derivative signs against a contour plot (U3-LO3)</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = x(y^2 + 1)</m>, whose contour plot is indicated in <xref ref="fig-8-5-contour-ex-3"/>. Five points are marked on the plot: <m>P = (0,0)</m>, <m>Q = (3/2,0)</m>, <m>R = (7/2,0)</m>, <m>S = (2,1)</m>, and <m>T = (2,-1)</m>. For each of these five points, determine from the contour plot whether <m>f_x</m> is positive, negative, or zero, and whether <m>f_y</m> is positive, negative, or zero. Then calculate these derivatives exactly and check whether your observations were correct.
			</p>

			<figure xml:id="fig-8-5-contour-ex-3">
				<caption>Contour plot for <m>f(x,y) = x(y^2 + 1)</m> with increments of <m>1</m>, and five points <m>P</m>, <m>Q</m>, <m>R</m>, <m>S</m>, and <m>T</m> indicated. Adapted from: Stanford's MATH 51 textbook</caption>
				<image source="figures/unit3/u3-contours-x-y2-plus-one-points.png" width="80%">
					<shortdescription>
						Contour plot of x times y squared plus one with five labeled points.
					</shortdescription>
					<description>
						<p>
							The contour plot shows level curves of <m>f(x,y)=x(y^2+1)</m> for levels from <m>0</m> through <m>10</m> in steps of <m>1</m>. The level curves are symmetric across the <m>x</m>-axis and bend to the right as <m>|y|</m> increases. The marked points are <m>P=(0,0)</m>, <m>Q=(3/2,0)</m>, <m>R=(7/2,0)</m>, <m>S=(2,1)</m>, and <m>T=(2,-1)</m>.
						</p>
					</description>
				</image>
			</figure>
		</statement>

		<solution>
			<p>
				At all five points, <m>f_x > 0</m> because the contour values increase as increase values of <m>x</m>. The contour lines around points <m>S</m> and <m>T</m> are more tightly packed than around <m>P</m>, <m>Q</m>, and <m>R</m>, indicating that <m>f_x</m> has larger magnitude at <m>S</m> and <m>T</m> than at <m>P</m>, <m>Q</m>, and <m>R</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				At the point <m>P</m>, <m>f_y = 0</m> since the contour line passing through <m>P</m> is a straight vertical line. It is not possible to determine the sign of <m>f_y</m> at the points <m>Q</m> and <m>R</m>, but we know the value of <m>f_y</m> must be small since the contour lines are more spread out than anywhere else in the plot. At the point <m>S</m>, <m>f_y > 0</m>, since the contour values increase as we increase in <m>y</m> values. At the point <m>T</m>, <m>f_y \lt 0</m> since the contour values <em>decrease</em>.
			</p>

			<p>
				We now verify these observations by computing partial derivatives. We check that
				<me>
					\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} = y^2 + 1 \quad\text{and}\quad \frac{\partial f}{\partial y} = 2xy
				</me>.
				So
				<ul>
					<li>
						<m>f_x(P) = f_x(0,0) = 1</m> and <m>f_y(P) = f_y(0,0) = 0</m>.
					</li>
					<li>
						<m>f_x(Q) = f_x(3/2,0) = 1</m> and <m>f_y(Q) = f_y(3/2,0) = 0</m>.
					</li>
					<li>
						<m>f_x(R) = f_x(7/2,0) = 1</m> and <m>f_y(R) = f_y(7/2,0) = 0</m>.
					</li>
					<li>
						<m>f_x(S) = f_x(2,1) = 2</m> and <m>f_y(S) = f_y(2,1) = 4</m>.
					</li>
					<li>
						<m>f_x(T) = f_x(2,-1) = 2</m> and <m>f_y(T) = f_y(2,-1) = -4</m>.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</activity>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection>
	<title>Partial derivatives with more variables</title>

	<p>
		The concept of partial derivatives extends naturally to functions of more than two variables.
	</p>

	<definition>
		<statement>
			<p>
				For a function <m>f(\mathbf{x}) = f(x_1, \dots, x_n)</m> of <m>n</m> variables, the partial derivative with respect to <m>x_i</m> is defined as
				<me>f_{x_i}(\mathbf{x}) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x_1, \dots, x_i+h, \dots, x_n) - f(x_1, \ldots, x_n)}{h}</me>
				provided this limit exists. Using vector notation, we can write this definition more cleanly, by writing
				<me>
					f_{x_i}(\mathbf{x}) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(\mathbf{x} + h \mathbf{e}_i) - f(\mathbf{x})}{h}
				</me>,
				where <m>\mathbf{e}_1,\dots,\mathbf{e}_n</m> are the standard basis vectors (recall <xref ref="def-2-2-standard-basis"/>). This partial derivative is also written as
				<me>
					\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_i} \quad \partial_{x_i} f \quad\text{or}\quad (D_i f)(\mathbf{x})
				</me>.
				If the variables <m>x_1,\dots,x_n</m> are denoted using different symbols, these symbols might also be used to denote the partial derivatives instead. For instance, in <m>\R^3</m>, we might write a function as <m>f(x,y,z)</m>, and then the three partial derivatives of <m>f</m> may be denoted using any of the following four notations:
				<ul>
					<li><m>
						\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m>, <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m>, and <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}</m>.</li>
						<li>
							<m>f_x, f_y</m>, and <m>f_z</m>.
						</li>
						<li>
							<m>\partial_x f, \partial_y f</m> and <m>\partial_z f</m>.
						</li>
						<li>
							<m>D_1 f</m>, <m>D_2 f</m>, and <m>D_3 f</m>.
						</li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>

		<p>
			As for functions of two variables, we can calculate these derivatives by treating all variables but that we are taking the partial derivative of as constants.
		</p>

		<activity>
      <title>Partial derivatives of a product of powers (U3-LO3)</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					If <m>f(x,y,z) = 5x^2y^2z^3</m>, find <m>f_x</m>, <m>f_y</m>, and <m>f_z</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					To find <m>f_x</m>, treat <m>y</m> and <m>z</m> as constants:
					<md>
						<mrow>f_x(x,y,z) \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(5x^2y^2z^3)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = 5(2x)y^2z^3</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = 10xy^2z^3</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>
					To find <m>f_y</m>, treat <m>x</m> and <m>z</m> as constants:
					<md>
						<mrow>f_y(x,y,z) \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(5x^2y^2z^3)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = 5x^2 (2y)z^3</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = 10x^2yz^3</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>
					To find <m>f_z</m>, treat <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> as constants:
					<md>
						<mrow>f_z(x,y,z) \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial z}(5x^2y^2z^3)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = 5x^2y^2(3z^2)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = 15x^2y^2z^2</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>

		<activity>
      <title>Partial derivatives of a three-variable polynomial (U3-LO3)</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find <m>f_x</m>, <m>f_y</m>, and <m>f_z</m> where <m>f(x,y,z) = xy + yz + xz</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>f_x(x,y,z) \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(xy + yz + xz)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = y + 0 + z</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = y + z</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>f_y(x,y,z) \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(xy + yz + xz)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = x + z + 0</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = x + z</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>f_z(x,y,z) \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial z}(xy + yz + xz)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = 0 + y + x</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = x + y</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>

		<activity>
      <title>Partial derivatives with logarithms and exponentials (U3-LO3)</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find <m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m> where <m>z = f(x,y) = x\ln y + ye^x</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>f_x(x,y) \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x\ln y + ye^x)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = \ln y + ye^x</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = \ln y + ye^x</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>f_y(x,y) \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(x\ln y + ye^x)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = x \cdot \frac{1}{y} + 1 \cdot e^x</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = \frac{x}{y} + e^x</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>

	</subsubsection>

	<subsubsection>
		<title>Higher-order partial derivatives</title>

		<p>
			Given a scalar-valued function <m>f</m>, the partial derivatives of <m>f</m> (where defined) are also scalar-valued functions, so we can consider partial derivatives of that function as well, which leads to <em>higher order partial derivatives</em>.
		</p>

		<definition>
			<statement>
				<p>
					If <m>f</m> is a function of two variables, then the <term>second-order partial derivatives</term> of <m>f</m> are defined as:

					<ul>
						<li>
							The derivative <m>f_{xx}</m> or <m>\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^2}</m>, obtained by taking the partial derivative of <m>f</m> twice in the <m>x</m> variable.
						</li>

						<li>
							The derivative <m>f_{xy}=(f_x)_y</m> or <m>\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial y \partial x}</m>, obtained by <em>first</em> differentiating <m>f</m> in the <m>x</m> variable, and <em>then</em> differentiating <m>f</m> in the <m>y</m> variable.
						</li>

						<li>
							The derivative <m>f_{yx}=(f_y)_x</m>, or <m>\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x \partial y}</m>, obtained by <em>first</em> differentiating <m>f</m> in the <m>y</m> variable, and <em>then</em> differentiating <m>f</m> in the <m>x</m> variable.
						</li>

						<li>
							The derivative <m>f_{yy}</m>, or <m>\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial y^2}</m>, obtained by differentiating <m>f</m> twice in the <m>y</m> variable.
						</li>
					</ul>
					More generally, for a function <m>f(x_1,\dots,x_n)</m>, the second-order partial derivatives are
					<me>f_{x_i x_j}=(f_{x_i})_{x_j}=\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x_j \partial x_i}</me>
					for <m>1 \leq i,j \leq n</m>, obtained by first differentiating <m>f</m> in the <m>x_i</m> variable, and then in the <m>x_j</m> variable. In operator notation, <m>f_{x_i x_j}=D_jD_i f</m>. Subscripts record the differentiation order from left to right; differential operators act from right to left.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>

		<theorem xml:id="thm-8-5-clairaut-theorem">
			<title>Clairaut's Theorem</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose a scalar-valued function <m>f</m> is defined in an open ball <m>B</m> that contains a point <m>\mathbf{a}</m>. If the functions <m>f_{x_i x_j}</m> and <m>f_{x_j x_i}</m> are well-defined and continuous on the ball <m>B</m>, then <m>f_{x_i x_j}(\mathbf{a}) = f_{x_j x_i}(\mathbf{a})</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</theorem>

		<activity>
      <title>First and second partial derivatives (U3-LO3)</title>
			<introduction>
				<p>
					In this activity we compute all four partial derivatives of the function <m>f(x,y) = x \ln y + y e^x</m>.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						First compute <m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m>.
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<p>
						We calculate that
						<me>
							f_x(x,y) = \ln y + y e^x
						</me>
						and
						<me>
							f_y(x,y) = x/y + e^x
						</me>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Next, compute <m>f_{xx}</m> and <m>f_{yy}</m>.
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<p>
						We calculate <m>f_{xx}</m> by differentiating the function <m>f_x(x,y) = \ln y + y e^x</m> in the <m>x</m>-variable, i.e.,
						<me>
							f_{xx}(x,y) = y e^x
						</me>.
						Similarly, we calculate <m>f_{yy}</m> by differentiating the function <m>f_y(x,y) = x/y + e^x</m> in the <m>y</m>-variable, i.e.,
						<me>
							f_{yy}(x,y) = -x/y^2
						</me>
					</p>.
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Now compute <m>f_{xy}</m> and <m>f_{yx}</m>.
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<p>
						We compute <m>f_{xy}</m> by differentiating the function <m>f_x = \ln y + y e^x</m> in the <m>y</m>-variable, so that
						<me>
							f_{xy}(x,y) = 1/y + e^x
						</me>.
						We compute <m>f_{yx}</m> by differentiating the function <m>f_y = x/y + e^x</m> in the <m>x</m>-variable, so that
						<me>
							f_{yx} = 1/y + e^x
						</me>.
						Notice that both functions are continuous, and equal to one another, as guaranteed by <xref ref="thm-8-5-clairaut-theorem"/>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</activity>

		<note>
			<statement>
				<p>
					For most functions <m>f</m> encountered in practice, the second-order partial derivatives are continuous where defined, and so <xref ref="thm-8-5-clairaut-theorem"/> applies. However, there are functions for which mixed partial derivatives are not equal to one another.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</note>

		<activity>
      <title>Second partial derivatives of a logarithm (U3-LO3)</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find all four second partial derivatives of the function
					<me>f(x,y) = \ln(x^3 + y^2)</me>.
				</p>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					We begin by calculating <m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m> using the chain rule, i.e., calculating that
					<me>
						f_x(x,y) = \frac{1}{x^3 + y^2} (3x^2) = \frac{3x^2}{x^3 + y^2}
					</me>
					and
					<me>
						f_y(x,y) = \frac{1}{x^3 + y^2} (2y) = \frac{2y}{x^3 + y^2}
					</me>.
					The quotient rule then tells us that
					<md>
						<mrow>
							f_{xx} \amp = (f_x)_x
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \frac{(x^3 + y^2) (6x) - (3x^2) ( 3x^2 )}{(x^3 + y^2)^2}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \frac{6x^4 + 6xy^2 - 9x^4}{(x^3 + y^2)^2}
						</mrow>
					</md>
					that
					<md>
						<mrow>
							f_{yy} \amp = (f_y)_y
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \frac{(x^3 + y^2) 2 - (2y)(2y)}{(x^3 + y^2)^2}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \frac{2x^3 - 2y^2}{(x^3 + y^2)^2}
						</mrow>
					</md>
					that
					<md>
						<mrow>
							f_{yx} \amp = (f_y)_x
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \frac{(x^3 + y^2) (0) - (2y)(3x^2)}{(x^3 + y^2)^2}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \frac{-6x^2y}{(x^3 + y^2)^2}
						</mrow>
					</md>
					and that
					<md>
						<mrow>
							f_{xy} \amp = (f_x)_y
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \frac{(x^3 + y^2)(0) - (3x^2)(2y)}{(x^3 + y^2)^2}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \frac{-6x^2 y}{(x^3 + y^2)^2}
						</mrow>
					</md>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>
    <activity xml:id="act-u3-sympy-second-partials">
      <title>Reading repeated and mixed derivatives in SymPy (U3-LO3)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Return to the polynomial in <xref ref="act-u3-sympy-first-partials"/>. The command <c>sp.diff(f, x, 2)</c> differentiates twice with respect to <c>x</c>. The command <c>sp.diff(f, x, y)</c> differentiates first with respect to <c>x</c>, then with respect to <c>y</c>; equivalently, it computes <c>sp.diff(sp.diff(f, x), y)</c>.
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import sympy as sp

x, y = sp.symbols("x y")
f = 3*x**2*y + 2*x - 3*y
dxx = sp.diff(f, x, 2)
d_x_then_y = sp.diff(f, x, y)
d_y_then_x = sp.diff(f, y, x)
dyy = sp.diff(f, y, 2)

print((dxx, d_x_then_y, d_y_then_x, dyy))
]]></code></program>
        <p>Output:</p>
        <program language="text"><code><![CDATA[(6*y, 6*x, 6*x, 0)
]]></code></program>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Match each output to <m>f_{xx}</m>, <m>f_{xy}</m>, <m>f_{yx}</m>, or <m>f_{yy}</m>, using this section's differentiation-order convention.</p></li>
          <li><p>Starting from <m>f_x=6xy+2</m> and <m>f_y=3x^2-3</m>, verify the four outputs by hand.</p></li>
          <li><p>Why does <xref ref="thm-8-5-clairaut-theorem"/> apply to this function? Does the matching output establish equality of mixed partial derivatives for every function?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          In order, the outputs are <m>f_{xx}=6y</m>, <m>f_{xy}=6x</m>, <m>f_{yx}=6x</m>, and <m>f_{yy}=0</m>. The leftmost subscript indicates the first differentiation: <c>d_x_then_y</c> represents <m>f_{xy}=(f_x)_y</m>, and <c>d_y_then_x</c> represents <m>f_{yx}=(f_y)_x</m>. Differentiating <m>6xy+2</m> in <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> gives <m>6y</m> and <m>6x</m>; differentiating <m>3x^2-3</m> in <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> gives <m>6x</m> and <m>0</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          This polynomial has continuous second partial derivatives everywhere, so Clairaut's theorem guarantees the equality. The output checks this particular function; it does not remove the theorem's hypotheses for other functions.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
	</subsubsection>

	<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-gradient-hessian">
		<title>Gradients and Hessians</title>

		<p>
			The gradient collects first partial derivatives of a scalar-valued function.
		</p>

			<definition xml:id="sec-8-5-gradient">
				<title>Gradient</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Let <m>f</m> be a scalar-valued function of <m>n</m> variables. At a point <m>\mathbf{x}</m> where all first partial derivatives of <m>f</m> exist, the <term>gradient</term> of <m>f</m> is the vector
					<me>
						(\nabla f)(\mathbf{x}) =
						\begin{bmatrix}
						\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x_1}(\mathbf{x}) \\
						\vdots \\
						\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x_n}(\mathbf{x})
						\end{bmatrix}
					</me>.
					When these vectors are defined throughout a domain, they form a <term>vector-valued function</term> <m>\nabla f</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					The symbol <m>\nabla</m>, called <q>nabla</q> (also called <q>del</q>), resembles an upside-down capital delta, <m>\Delta</m>; its name comes from a Greek word for a harp. We read <m>\nabla f</m> as <q>the gradient of <m>f</m>.</q>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>

		<activity xml:id="act-8-5-grad-ex-12">
      <title>The gradient of a trigonometric function (U3-LO3)</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>f(x,y) = \sin(x + y^2)</m>. Find the gradient of <m>f</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					We use the chain rule and the derivative of sine from <xref ref="tab-u3-derivative-reminders"/> in <xref ref="sec-u3-general-functions-domains"/>. To differentiate the inner expression <m>x+y^2</m>, we use the sum, power, and constant rules from the same table.
				</p>
				<p>
					Holding <m>y</m> fixed makes <m>y^2</m> a constant, so
					<md>
						<mrow>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x,y) \amp = \cos(x+y^2)\dfrac{\partial}{\partial x}(x+y^2)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = \cos(x+y^2)(1+0)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = \cos(x+y^2).</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
				<p>
					Holding <m>x</m> fixed instead makes <m>x</m> a constant, so
					<md>
						<mrow>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(x,y) \amp = \cos(x+y^2)\dfrac{\partial}{\partial y}(x+y^2)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = \cos(x+y^2)(0+2y)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = 2y\cos(x+y^2).</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
				<p>
					Collecting these partial derivatives gives
					<me>
						\nabla f(x,y) = \begin{bmatrix} \cos(x + y^2) \\ 2y \cos(x + y^2) \end{bmatrix}
					</me>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>

		<activity xml:id="act-8-5-nabla-f-ex">
      <title>Evaluating a gradient at a point (U3-LO3)</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + xy + y^2</m>. Compute <m>\nabla f(1,2)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					We use the sum, power, constant-multiple, and constant rules from <xref ref="tab-u3-derivative-reminders"/> in <xref ref="sec-u3-general-functions-domains"/>. Each partial derivative treats the other input as a constant.
				</p>
				<p>
					Holding <m>y</m> fixed, the term <m>xy</m> is a constant multiple of <m>x</m>, and <m>y^2</m> is constant. Thus
					<md>
						<mrow>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x,y) \amp = \dfrac{\partial}{\partial x}(x^2)+y\dfrac{\partial}{\partial x}(x)+\dfrac{\partial}{\partial x}(y^2)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = 2x+y(1)+0</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = 2x+y.</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
				<p>
					Holding <m>x</m> fixed, the term <m>x^2</m> is constant, and <m>xy</m> is a constant multiple of <m>y</m>. Thus
					<md>
						<mrow>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(x,y) \amp = \dfrac{\partial}{\partial y}(x^2)+x\dfrac{\partial}{\partial y}(y)+\dfrac{\partial}{\partial y}(y^2)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = 0+x(1)+2y</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp = x+2y.</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
				<p>
					Collecting these partial derivatives gives
					<me>
						\nabla f(x,y) = \begin{bmatrix} 2x + y \\ x + 2y \end{bmatrix}
					</me>
					and in particular,
					<me>
						\nabla f(1,2) = \begin{bmatrix} 2(1) + (2) \\ (1) + 2(2) \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}
					</me>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>

		<p>
			The Hessian collects second partial derivatives.
		</p>

		<definition xml:id="def-8-5-hessian">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>f</m> be a scalar-valued function of <m>n</m> variables, and suppose the second partial derivatives of <m>f</m> exist at a point <m>\mathbf{x}</m>. The <term>Hessian matrix</term> of <m>f</m> at <m>\mathbf{x}</m> is the <m>n \times n</m> matrix of second partial derivatives:
					<me>
						H_f(\mathbf{x}) = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x_1^2}(\mathbf{x}) \amp \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x_2 \partial x_1}(\mathbf{x}) \amp \cdots \amp \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x_n \partial x_1}(\mathbf{x}) \\ \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x_1 \partial x_2}(\mathbf{x}) \amp \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x_2^2}(\mathbf{x}) \amp \cdots \amp \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x_n \partial x_2}(\mathbf{x}) \\ \vdots \amp \vdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \\ \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x_1 \partial x_n}(\mathbf{x}) \amp \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x_2 \partial x_n}(\mathbf{x}) \amp \cdots \amp \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x_n^2}(\mathbf{x}) \end{bmatrix}
					</me>
					That is, <m>H_f(\mathbf{x})</m> has <m>(i,j)</m>-entry <m>f_{x_i x_j}(\mathbf{x})</m>, obtained by first differentiating in the <m>x_i</m> variable, and then in the <m>x_j</m> variable. Row <m>i</m> differentiates <m>f_{x_i}</m> with respect to each input variable.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>

		<remark xml:id="rem-u3-hessian-symmetry">
			<title>Symmetry of the Hessian</title>
			<p>
				When all mixed second partial derivatives are continuous on a ball around <m>\mathbf{x}</m>, the Hessian is <xref ref="def-symmetric-skew-symmetric" text="custom">symmetric</xref> at <m>\mathbf{x}</m>. Indeed, <xref ref="thm-8-5-clairaut-theorem"/> gives, for each pair <m>i \ne j</m>,
				<me>
					\bigl(H_f(\mathbf{x})\bigr)_{ij}
					= \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x_j\partial x_i}(\mathbf{x})
					= \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x_i\partial x_j}(\mathbf{x})
					= \bigl(H_f(\mathbf{x})\bigr)_{ji}.
				</me>
				Thus entries reflected across the main diagonal are equal. Transposing swaps these entries and leaves the diagonal unchanged, so
				<me>H_f(\mathbf{x})^T = H_f(\mathbf{x}),</me>
				which is precisely the defining condition for a symmetric matrix.
			</p>
		</remark>

		<p>
			In particular, the Hessian of a scalar-valued function of two variables can be written
			<me>
				H_f = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^2} \amp \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial y \partial x} \\ \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x \partial y} \amp \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial y^2} \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} f_{xx} \amp f_{xy} \\ f_{yx} \amp f_{yy} \end{bmatrix}
			</me>.
		</p>

		<activity>
      <title>The Hessian of a trigonometric function (U3-LO3)</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the Hessian matrix for the function
					<me>
						f(x,y) = \sin(x + y^2)
					</me>
					at a general point <m>(x,y)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					We start by computing the first and second partial derivatives of <m>f</m>. In <xref ref="act-8-5-grad-ex-12"/> we computed that
					<md>
						<mrow>f_x(x,y) \amp = \cos(x + y^2),</mrow>
						<mrow>f_y(x,y) \amp = 2y \cos(x + y^2)</mrow>
					</md>.
					We then calculate that
					<md>
						<mrow>
							f_{xx} \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \{ f_x \}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = - \sin(x + y^2)
						</mrow>
					</md>,
					that
					<md>
						<mrow>
							f_{yy} \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \{ f_y \}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \{ 2y \cos(x + y^2) \}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = 2 \cos(x + y^2) - 2y \sin(x + y^2) (2y)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = 2 \cos(x + y^2) - 4y^2 \sin(x + y^2)
						</mrow>
					</md>.
				</p>
				<p>
					For <m>f_{yx}</m>, we differentiate <m>f_y</m> with respect to <m>x</m>, holding <m>y</m> fixed. Thus <m>2y</m> is a constant factor. We use the constant-multiple and chain rules, together with the derivative of cosine, from <xref ref="tab-u3-derivative-reminders"/> in <xref ref="sec-u3-general-functions-domains"/>. The sum and constant rules give <m>\frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x+y^2)=1+0</m>:
					<md>
						<mrow>
							f_{yx} \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \{ f_y \}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \{ 2y \cos(x + y^2) \}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = 2y\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\{\cos(x+y^2)\}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = 2y[-\sin(x+y^2)]\frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x+y^2)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = -2y\sin(x+y^2)(1+0)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = -2y \sin(x + y^2)
						</mrow>
					</md>.
				</p>
				<p>
					For <m>f_{xy}</m>, we differentiate <m>f_x</m> with respect to <m>y</m>, holding <m>x</m> fixed. The chain rule and the derivative of cosine in <xref ref="tab-u3-derivative-reminders"/> give the outer derivative; the sum, constant, and power rules give the inner derivative <m>\frac{\partial}{\partial y}(x+y^2)=0+2y</m>:
					<md>
						<mrow>
							f_{xy} \amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \{ f_x \}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \{ \cos(x + y^2) \}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = - \sin(x + y^2)(2y)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = -2y \sin(x + y^2)
						</mrow>
					</md>.
					Thus
					<md>
						<mrow>
							\amp H_f(x,y) = \begin{bmatrix} f_{xx} \amp f_{xy} \\ f_{yx} \amp f_{yy} \end{bmatrix}
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \begin{bmatrix} - \sin(x + y^2) \amp - 2y \sin(x + y^2) \\ -2y \sin(x + y^2) \amp \begin{gathered}2 \cos(x + y^2) \\ {}- 4y^2\sin(x + y^2)\end{gathered} \end{bmatrix}
						</mrow>
					</md>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>

		<activity>
      <title>The Hessian of a quadratic function (U3-LO3)</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the Hessian matrix of the function
					<me>f(x,y) = x^2 + xy + y^2</me>
					at a general point <m>(x,y)</m>
				</p>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					We compute that
					<me>
						f_{xx} = \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \{ 2x + y \} = 2,
					</me>
					that
					<me>
						f_{xy} = \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \{ 2x + y \} = 1
					</me>,
					that
					<me>
						f_{yx} = \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \{ x + 2y \} = 1
					</me>,
					and that
					<me>
						f_{yy} = \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \{ x + 2y \} = 2
					</me>.
					So
					<me>
						H_f(x,y) = \begin{bmatrix} f_{xx} \amp f_{xy} \\ f_{yx} \amp f_{yy} \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}
					</me>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>

		<activity>
      <title>A gradient and Hessian in three variables (U3-LO3)</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>f(x,y,z) = xy + yz + xz</m>. Compute its gradient <m>\nabla f</m> and Hessian <m>H_f</m> at a general point <m>(x,y,z)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					We begin by computing the gradient. We calculate that
					<me>
						f_x(x,y,z) = y + 0 + z
					</me>,
					that
					<me>
						f_y(x,y,z) = x + z + 0
					</me>,
					and that
					<me>
						f_z(x,y,z) = 0 + y + x
					</me>.
					So
					<me>\nabla f(x,y,z) = \begin{bmatrix} y + z \\ x + z \\ y + x \end{bmatrix}</me>.
				</p>

				<p>
					Next, we compute the second derivatives of <m>f</m>. We compute that <m>f_{xx} = f_{yy} = f_{zz} = 0</m>, and that <m>f_{xy} = f_{xz} = f_{yx} = f_{yz} = f_{zx} = f_{zy} = 1</m>. So
					<me>
						H_f(x,y,z) = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
					</me>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</activity>
    <activity xml:id="act-u3-sympy-gradient-hessian">
      <title>Building a gradient and Hessian in SymPy (U3-LO3)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Collect the derivatives of <m>f(x,y)=3x^2y+2x-3y</m> into a vector and a matrix. As in Unit 2, <c>sp.Matrix([a, b])</c> creates a column matrix and <c>.shape</c> reports its numbers of rows and columns. The command <c>sp.hessian(f, (x, y))</c> constructs the Hessian with coordinates ordered as <m>x,y</m>.
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import sympy as sp

x, y = sp.symbols("x y")
f = 3*x**2*y + 2*x - 3*y
grad_f = sp.Matrix([sp.diff(f, x), sp.diff(f, y)])
H = sp.hessian(f, (x, y))

print(grad_f)
print(H)
print((grad_f.shape, H.shape))
]]></code></program>
        <p>Output:</p>
        <program language="text"><code><![CDATA[Matrix([[6*x*y + 2], [3*x**2 - 3]])
Matrix([[6*y, 6*x], [6*x, 0]])
((2, 1), (2, 2))
]]></code></program>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Write the gradient and Hessian in mathematical notation. Why is one a <m>2\times1</m> column and the other a <m>2\times2</m> matrix?</p></li>
          <li><p>Predict <c>grad_f.subs({x: 1, y: 2})</c> and <c>H.subs({x: 1, y: 2})</c>. Does substitution change either shape?</p></li>
          <li><p>What derivative is stored in <c>H[0, 1]</c>? Explain the symmetry of <c>H</c> and why its zero bottom-right entry does not mean that <m>f_y</m> is identically zero.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The gradient collects two first partial derivatives, while the Hessian collects the four second partial derivatives:
          <me>\nabla f(x,y)=\begin{bmatrix}6xy+2\\3x^2-3\end{bmatrix},\qquad H_f(x,y)=\begin{bmatrix}6y&amp;6x\\6x&amp;0\end{bmatrix}.</me>
          At the specified point,
          <me>\nabla f(1,2)=\begin{bmatrix}14\\0\end{bmatrix},\qquad H_f(1,2)=\begin{bmatrix}12&amp;6\\6&amp;0\end{bmatrix}.</me>
          Evaluating entries leaves the shapes <m>2\times1</m> and <m>2\times2</m> unchanged.
        </p>
        <p>
          SymPy uses zero-based indices, so <c>H[0, 1]</c> is the first-row, second-column entry, <m>f_{xy}=6x</m>. The mixed partial derivatives agree because the polynomial satisfies Clairaut's theorem. The zero entry is <m>f_{yy}</m>: <m>f_y=3x^2-3</m> is independent of <m>y</m>, but it is not the zero function.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity xml:id="act-u3-prediction-loss-derivatives">
      <title>How model weights change the loss (U3-LO3)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For the fixed data in <xref ref="act-u3-prediction-loss-model"/>,
          <me>\mathcal L(w_1,w_2)=(w_1-1)^2+(w_2-2)^2+(w_1+w_2-2)^2.</me>
          We will continue to use the starting weights <m>\mathbf{w}_0=(1,1)</m>.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Find both first partial derivatives and assemble <m>\nabla\mathcal L</m>. Evaluate it at <m>\mathbf{w}_0</m>. Interpret each component while holding the other weight fixed.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute the Hessian <m>H_{\mathcal L}</m>. Verify by matrix multiplication that it equals <m>2X^TX</m> for the data matrix <m>X</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>The first partial derivative in <m>w_1</m> vanishes at <m>\mathbf{w}_0</m>. Does this mean the loss is unchanged whenever we change <m>w_1</m>? Check by expanding <m>\mathcal L(1+t,1)</m>.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p>You can check your derivative formulas using the SymPy commands in <xref ref="act-u3-sympy-gradient-hessian"/>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Differentiating one weight at a time gives
              <md>
                <mrow>\mathcal L_{w_1}(\mathbf{w})\amp=2(w_1-1)+2(w_1+w_2-2)</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp=4w_1+2w_2-6,</mrow>
                <mrow>\mathcal L_{w_2}(\mathbf{w})\amp=2(w_2-2)+2(w_1+w_2-2)</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp=2w_1+4w_2-8.</mrow>
              </md>
              Thus
              <md>
                <mrow>\nabla\mathcal L(\mathbf{w})\amp=\begin{bmatrix}4w_1+2w_2-6\\2w_1+4w_2-8\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
                <mrow>\nabla\mathcal L(1,1)\amp=\begin{bmatrix}0\\-2\end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
              </md>
              At these weights, the instantaneous rate of change in <m>w_1</m> is zero when <m>w_2=1</m> is fixed. Increasing <m>w_2</m> slightly while keeping <m>w_1=1</m> decreases the loss at a rate of <m>2</m> units of loss per unit increase in <m>w_2</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Differentiate each first partial derivative with respect to each weight, holding the other weight fixed:
              <md>
                <mrow>\mathcal L_{w_1w_1}(\mathbf{w})\amp=\frac{\partial}{\partial w_1}(4w_1+2w_2-6)=4,</mrow>
                <mrow>\mathcal L_{w_1w_2}(\mathbf{w})\amp=\frac{\partial}{\partial w_2}(4w_1+2w_2-6)=2,</mrow>
                <mrow>\mathcal L_{w_2w_1}(\mathbf{w})\amp=\frac{\partial}{\partial w_1}(2w_1+4w_2-8)=2,</mrow>
                <mrow>\mathcal L_{w_2w_2}(\mathbf{w})\amp=\frac{\partial}{\partial w_2}(2w_1+4w_2-8)=4.</mrow>
              </md>
              These four second partial derivatives are constant, so the Hessian is
              <me>
                H_{\mathcal L}(\mathbf{w})=\begin{bmatrix}4\amp2\\2\amp4\end{bmatrix}
                =2\begin{bmatrix}1\amp0\amp1\\0\amp1\amp1\end{bmatrix}
                \begin{bmatrix}1\amp0\\0\amp1\\1\amp1\end{bmatrix}
                =2X^TX.
              </me>
              Its entries describe how the first partial derivatives change as the weights change.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Direct substitution gives <m>\mathcal L(1+t,1)=t^2+(-1)^2+t^2=1+2t^2</m>. The zero partial derivative describes the rate at <m>t=0</m>; the quadratic term changes the loss for every nonzero <m>t</m>. This is not an exactly forgotten direction of the prediction map from <xref ref="u2-anatomy-linear-map" text="custom">Unit 2</xref>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </solution>
    </activity>
	</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-more-on-jacobian-matrix">
			<title>Jacobian matrices</title>

			<p>
				For vector-valued maps, the Jacobian matrix collects the partial derivatives of all component functions.
			</p>

			<p>
				The rows correspond to component functions. The columns describe how the output changes when one input coordinate changes.
			</p>

<definition>
				<title>Jacobian matrix</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						For a vector-valued map <m>F: \R^n \to \R^m</m> given by
						<me>
							F(\mathbf{x}) = \begin{bmatrix} F_1(\mathbf{x}) \\ F_2(\mathbf{x}) \\ \vdots \\ F_m(\mathbf{x}) \end{bmatrix}
						</me>
						where <m>\mathbf{x} = (x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n)</m>, the <term>Jacobian matrix</term> of <m>F</m> is the <m>m \times n</m> matrix:

						<me>
							J_F(\mathbf{x}) =
							\begin{bmatrix}
							\frac{\partial F_1}{\partial x_1} \amp \frac{\partial F_1}{\partial x_2} \amp \ldots \amp \frac{\partial F_1}{\partial x_n} \\
							\frac{\partial F_2}{\partial x_1} \amp \frac{\partial F_2}{\partial x_2} \amp \ldots \amp \frac{\partial F_2}{\partial x_n} \\
							\vdots \amp \vdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \\
							\frac{\partial F_m}{\partial x_1} \amp \frac{\partial F_m}{\partial x_2} \amp \ldots \amp \frac{\partial F_m}{\partial x_n}
							\end{bmatrix}
						</me>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</definition>

			<p>
				The Jacobian matrix generalizes the concept of the derivative to a vector-valued map of multiple variables. It provides a compact representation of all first-order partial derivatives of a function.
			</p>

			<activity>
        <title>The Jacobian matrix of a scalar-valued function (U3-LO3)</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Find the Jacobian matrix of <m>f(x,y,z) = xy + yz + xz</m>.
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<p>
						First compute each partial derivative, holding the other two variables fixed:
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x,y,z) \amp= \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(xy+yz+xz)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= y+0+z=y+z,</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}(x,y,z) \amp= \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(xy+yz+xz)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= x+z+0=x+z,</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}(x,y,z) \amp= \frac{\partial}{\partial z}(xy+yz+xz)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0+y+x=x+y.</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					<p>
						For a scalar-valued function <m>f: \R^n \to \R</m>, the Jacobian matrix is the transpose of the gradient, i.e., <m>J_f = (\nabla f)^T</m>. Place the three partial derivatives in a row, in the variable order <m>x,y,z</m>:
						<md>
							<mrow>J_f(x,y,z) \amp= \begin{bmatrix} \dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x,y,z) \amp \dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(x,y,z) \amp \dfrac{\partial f}{\partial z}(x,y,z) \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \begin{bmatrix} y + z \amp x + z \amp x + y \end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</activity>

			<activity>
        <title>The Jacobian matrix of a three-component map (U3-LO3)</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Compute the Jacobian matrix of <m>F(x,y) = ( x^2 - y^2, 2xy, x+y )</m>.
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<p>
						We compute that
						<md>
							<mrow>
								J_F \amp = \begin{bmatrix} \dfrac{\partial F_1}{\partial x} \amp \dfrac{\partial F_1}{\partial y} \\[6pt] \dfrac{\partial F_2}{\partial x} \amp \dfrac{\partial F_2}{\partial y} \\[6pt] \dfrac{\partial F_3}{\partial x} \amp \dfrac{\partial F_3}{\partial y} \end{bmatrix}
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\amp = \begin{bmatrix} 2x \amp -2y \\ 2y \amp 2x \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
							</mrow>
						</md>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</activity>

			<activity>
        <title>The Jacobian matrix of a gradient (U3-LO3)</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Compute the Jacobian matrix of the gradient function <m>\nabla f(x,y)</m> for <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + xy + y^2</m>.
					</p>
				</statement>

				<solution>
					<p>
						First, let's compute the gradient of <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + xy + y^2</m>:
						<md>
							<mrow>
								\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} \amp= 2x + y
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\frac{\partial f}{\partial y} \amp= x + 2y
							</mrow>
						</md>
						So <m>\nabla f(x,y) = \begin{bmatrix} 2x + y \\ x + 2y \end{bmatrix}</m>.
						The Jacobian matrix of the gradient is
						<md>
							<mrow>
								J_{\nabla f} \amp = \begin{bmatrix} \dfrac{\partial (\nabla f)_1}{\partial x} \amp \dfrac{\partial (\nabla f)_1}{\partial y} \\[6pt] \dfrac{\partial (\nabla f)_2}{\partial x} \amp \dfrac{\partial (\nabla f)_2}{\partial y} \end{bmatrix}
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\amp = \begin{bmatrix} \dfrac{\partial}{\partial x}(2x+y) \amp \dfrac{\partial}{\partial y}(2x+y) \\[6pt] \dfrac{\partial}{\partial x}(x+2y) \amp \dfrac{\partial}{\partial y}(x+2y) \end{bmatrix}
							</mrow>
							<mrow>
								\amp = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}
							</mrow>
						</md>.
						This is the <em>Hessian</em> of <m>f</m> (recall <xref ref="def-8-5-hessian"/>). In general, the <m>(i,j)</m>-entry of <m>J_{\nabla f}</m> is <m>(f_{x_i})_{x_j}=f_{x_i x_j}</m>, so <m>J_{\nabla f}=H_f</m> wherever these entries exist.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</activity>
    <note xml:id="note-u3-jacobian-gradient-hessian">
      <title>Jacobian, gradient, and Hessian</title>
      <p>
        For a scalar-valued function <m>f:\R^n\to\R</m>, the Jacobian has one row, while the gradient in <xref ref="sec-8-5-gradient"/> is a column vector. Wherever the first partial derivatives exist,
        <me>
          J_f(\mathbf{x})
          =\begin{bmatrix}\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x_1}(\mathbf{x}) \amp \cdots \amp \dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x_n}(\mathbf{x})\end{bmatrix}
          =\bigl(\nabla f(\mathbf{x})\bigr)^T.
        </me>
        Equivalently, <m>\nabla f(\mathbf{x})=J_f(\mathbf{x})^T</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the second partial derivatives also exist, the Jacobian of the gradient is the Hessian:
        <me>J_{\nabla f}(\mathbf{x})=H_f(\mathbf{x}).</me>
        To see this, the <m>i</m>th component of <m>\nabla f</m> is <m>\partial f/\partial x_i</m>. Row <m>i</m>, column <m>j</m> of its Jacobian therefore contains
        <md>
          <mrow>\bigl[J_{\nabla f}(\mathbf{x})\bigr]_{ij} \amp =\dfrac{\partial}{\partial x_j}\left(\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x_i}\right)(\mathbf{x})</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp =\dfrac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x_j\partial x_i}(\mathbf{x})=\bigl[H_f(\mathbf{x})\bigr]_{ij},</mrow>
        </md>
        by <xref ref="def-8-5-hessian"/>. Thus <m>J_f</m> has shape <m>1\times n</m>, <m>\nabla f</m> has shape <m>n\times1</m>, and <m>J_{\nabla f}=H_f</m> has shape <m>n\times n</m>.
      </p>
    </note>

    <activity xml:id="act-u3-sympy-jacobian-of-gradient">
      <title>Checking the Jacobian of a gradient in SymPy (U3-LO3)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The command <c>F.jacobian([x, y])</c> differentiates each component of a SymPy column matrix <c>F</c>. Rows follow the component order in <c>F</c>; columns follow the variable order <c>[x, y]</c>. Compare the scalar-valued function <m>f(x,y)=3x^2y+2x-3y</m> with its vector-valued gradient.
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import sympy as sp

x, y = sp.symbols("x y")
f = 3*x**2*y + 2*x - 3*y
grad_f = sp.Matrix([sp.diff(f, x), sp.diff(f, y)])
J_f = sp.Matrix([f]).jacobian([x, y])
J_grad = grad_f.jacobian([x, y])
H = sp.hessian(f, (x, y))

print(J_f)
print(J_grad)
print(J_grad - H)
]]></code></program>
        <p>Output:</p>
        <program language="text"><code><![CDATA[Matrix([[6*x*y + 2, 3*x**2 - 3]])
Matrix([[6*y, 6*x], [6*x, 0]])
Matrix([[0, 0], [0, 0]])
]]></code></program>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Why does <c>J_f</c> have one row, while <c>J_grad</c> has two? Give the input and output dimensions of each map.</p></li>
          <li><p>How is <c>J_f</c> related to <c>grad_f</c>? Interpret the first row and the second column of <c>J_grad</c>.</p></li>
          <li><p>What identity does the zero matrix verify? Is this a comparison at one point or a symbolic comparison for this polynomial?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The map <m>f:\R^2\to\R</m> has one component, so <m>J_f</m> has shape <m>1\times2</m>. The map <m>\nabla f:\R^2\to\R^2</m> has two components, so <m>J_{\nabla f}</m> has shape <m>2\times2</m>. The scalar Jacobian is the transpose of the gradient: <m>J_f=(\nabla f)^T</m>, represented by <c>grad_f.T</c>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first row of <m>J_{\nabla f}</m> differentiates the first component <m>f_x=6xy+2</m> with respect to <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. Its second column differentiates both components with respect to <m>y</m>. These give the row <m>\begin{bmatrix}6y&amp;6x\end{bmatrix}</m> and the column <m>\begin{bmatrix}6x\\0\end{bmatrix}</m>, respectively.
        </p>
        <p>
          The zero difference verifies <m>J_{\nabla f}=H_f</m> for this polynomial. No point was substituted: every entry of the symbolic difference is zero for all <m>(x,y)\in\R^2</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
		</subsubsection>
</subsection>
